{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/stripe-sequence-validation.md", "content": "# Uniform stripe sequence tasks: validation and revision

For grids made of full-width, single-color rows, collapse each grid to a 1-D sequence of row colors before inferring the transformation. Compare input and output sequences position by position, including leading and trailing background rows and every gap between visible stripes. Check whether output preserves anchor rows, repeats a motif, extends a sequence, or uses a phase determined by the first/last marker. Do not infer a 2-D periodic tiling merely because other tasks in the broader benchmark use diagonal traces; select the transformation family supported by the current demos.

After a wrong answer, binary feedback rejects the whole candidate but gives no localization. Re-evaluate the rule itself against all demos rather than making repeated near-duplicate grids. Before serializing, validate the output dimensions, count every row's width, and ensure all cell values are valid integers 0–9.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/stripe-sequence-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/noisy-background-pattern-propagation.md", "content": "# Noisy-background pattern propagation checkpoint

When a small colored motif is embedded in a mostly two-color noisy field and outputs add that motif color at many scattered locations, do not infer a rule from visual resemblance alone. Compare every input-output pair cell by cell and form the exact changed-cell mask. Test exact motif placements, orientations, overlaps, and near-matches; conspicuous uncolored near-matches are negative evidence that a candidate selection rule must explain. Check that original marker cells are preserved and that all additions—not merely many of them—follow one consistent criterion. Before requesting another demo, state at least two surviving rules, their distinct predictions for the active test, and the concrete example outcome that would discriminate them. A further noisy grid without such a discriminator is not an evidence-led request.

This is a general audit method, not a claim that motif-copying is the right explanation for every noisy grid. See also `noisy-region-hypothesis-audit.md` and `overlapping-pattern-copy-audit.md` for connectivity and false-positive placement pitfalls.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-checkpoint/references/noisy-background-pattern-propagation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Reconcile heterogeneous or seemingly contradictory demos

With costly demos that vary in size, palette, and visual structure, compare each input/output pair at the exact changed-cell level before choosing an action. Record preserved cells/regions and unchanged pairs as constraints. Normalize semantic color roles only where the edits support the mapping; superficial resemblance to a known task or a remembered reference is not validation.

Test a proposed shared rule against every example, separating trigger, selected extent, and replacement mapping. If some pairs appear incompatible, state that uncertainty instead of ignoring inconvenient examples. After a rejected submission, reject the whole compound hypothesis and rebuild the evidence ledger; do not switch transformation families based solely on unrelated skill notes or make unsupported local edits. Spend another demo only when explicit surviving alternatives predict materially different test outputs and the information justifies its cost. Maintain the required single-action JSON protocol.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/repeated-noisy-task-failure.md", "content": "# Repeated failure and evidence discipline

In a costly continual-ARC task, many demonstrations and repeated binary rejections can still fail to identify the rule if the examples are not explicitly reconciled. Before the next action after each pair, record what changed and what stayed fixed across all demonstrations, separating trigger, transformed extent, direction, color mapping, and exceptions. Compare normalized geometry across demos even when grid sizes and palettes differ.

A wrong submission rejects the whole candidate transformation; it does not identify a faulty cell. After rejection, materially revise the structural hypothesis and validate its predictions against every demo. Do not cycle through unmodified-input answers, arbitrary single-cell changes, or unsupported remembered patterns. When demos are exhausted, use remaining attempts for distinct hypotheses that explain the evidence, rather than cosmetic variants.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/repeated-noisy-task-failure.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/binary-feedback-and-mixed-demos.md", "content": "# Binary feedback and heterogeneous demos

A demo stream may vary grid dimensions and colors while illustrating a shared transformation. Compare each pair as a complete constraint set: record all changed and preserved cells, boundaries, propagation direction, and where filling stops. Do not assume unrelated palette changes mean a different task.

After an incorrect submission, treat the result as evidence against the submitted rule/grid. State internally what assumption failed, revisit demo differences, and change the candidate meaningfully before trying again. Avoid submitting an identical grid repeatedly. Binary feedback doesn't identify the correct cell, so use the existing demonstrations to narrow alternatives; spend remaining demo requests only when a new example can discriminate among live hypotheses.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/binary-feedback-and-mixed-demos.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/failed-noisy-grid-inference.md", "content": "# Failed noisy-grid inference: preserve full changed-cell evidence

This attempt stream involved heterogeneous noisy-background demonstrations and a compact third-color motif. Multiple unsupported test candidates were submitted: recoloring the marker-adjacent cells, 4-/8-connected zero components, enclosed zero cells, and finally leaving the grid unchanged. All were rejected. The demos had visibly different changed masks and did not establish a simple uniform flood-fill rule; connectivity and proximity alone were not sufficient evidence.

Reusable lesson: compare every demo's changed and preserved cells before selecting a transformation. Explicitly test competing neighborhood/connectivity rules across complete pairs and inspect exceptions; do not infer global propagation merely from a marker in one example. After binary rejection, discard the whole candidate, not just a guessed boundary. Once the demo cap is exhausted, avoid cycling through local variants or reverting to input unchanged without evidence. This note records failed candidates, not a validated alternative rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/failed-noisy-grid-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/post-rejection-hypothesis-reset.md", "content": "# Reset after binary rejection

A wrong submission falsifies the complete proposed transformation; binary feedback gives no cell-level diagnosis. Before using another attempt, rebuild a compact ledger from every demo: changed and preserved locations or structures, normalized color roles, candidate trigger, selected extent, replacement mapping, and exceptions.

Require the next grid to come from a materially revised rule that is tested against all demonstrations. Cosmetic edits to nearby cells, another broad recoloring variant, or simply reverting to the input are not meaningful revisions. Once the demo cap is exhausted, select the best-supported candidate from the ledger rather than cycling unverified guesses just because attempts remain. Keep the strict one-action JSON protocol.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/post-rejection-hypothesis-reset.md"}
